WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.593
New 3D and building tools say an agent can drive them.

00:00:03.593 --> 00:00:06.124
Which of them can do anything with a scan?

00:00:10.915 --> 00:00:11.690
Hi, I'm Figaro!

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.013
I read the code of eight of these tools.

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:17.111
Let's place them on the road from capture to model, and open

00:00:17.111 --> 00:00:18.144
the best one up.

00:00:19.508 --> 00:00:20.093
Chapter one.

00:00:20.093 --> 00:00:21.262
Where a scan goes.

00:00:26.358 --> 00:00:28.414
A scan to building job has five stops.

00:00:29.503 --> 00:00:32.507
First you capture the site, with a scanner, a three sixty

00:00:32.507 --> 00:00:33.600
camera or a phone.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.036
Then you build a dense point cloud, or a mesh, or a splat.

00:00:37.456 --> 00:00:40.646
Then walls, slabs and openings get traced over that reference.

00:00:40.646 --> 00:00:43.517
This is the thin step for every tool here.

00:00:43.937 --> 00:00:47.437
Then they become a building model, with types and parameters, written out

00:00:47.437 --> 00:00:48.603
as an IFC file.

00:00:49.023 --> 00:00:52.271
Last, you ship it to a viewer, a client or a renderer.

00:00:52.271 --> 00:00:54.706
Most of these tools crowd the last two stops.

00:00:56.508 --> 00:01:00.040
The one working loop from capture to model is not a product.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.571
A creator filmed a shrine with a three sixty camera, built a

00:01:03.571 --> 00:01:06.514
dense point cloud, and handed it to Claude in Blender.

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:09.328
Measured points for position, the images for meaning.

00:01:09.328 --> 00:01:12.919
On an earlier street scene, a splat from the same kind of

00:01:12.919 --> 00:01:15.911
data had too many floaters, so the recipe dropped it.

00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:18.057
Chapter two.

00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:19.742
A kernel that holds records.

00:01:24.142 --> 00:01:26.932
Mutiny BIM is the most serious of the set.

00:01:26.932 --> 00:01:30.652
Its core keeps records and commits, each trade is an add-on, and

00:01:30.652 --> 00:01:33.442
every edit is a command the server runs again.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.024
Here is the formula.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.443
A parameter is just a key with a unit.

00:01:39.443 --> 00:01:41.703
If the element sets it, that value wins.

00:01:42.123 --> 00:01:44.776
If not, the value comes from the element's type.

00:01:44.776 --> 00:01:47.428
Clear the override, and the type's value comes back.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:53.490
A trade is a pack: a manifest, plus contributions to versioned extension

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.984
points on one contract.

00:01:55.404 --> 00:01:59.299
Inside, the example pack adds a class for a retention vault, and

00:01:59.299 --> 00:02:01.247
a parameter for its required volume.

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:04.921
And a lint rule that flags an undersized vault, and a

00:02:04.921 --> 00:02:06.104
calculation that sizes it.

00:02:06.524 --> 00:02:11.188
The model pins the pack's exact version, and IFC export reads the

00:02:11.188 --> 00:02:13.908
registries, so the new parameter exports unchanged.

00:02:15.758 --> 00:02:16.489
Chapter three.

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:17.952
Every edit runs twice.

00:02:22.758 --> 00:02:25.047
The agent does not get a hundred tools.

00:02:25.047 --> 00:02:28.481
It gets nine, and writes a short script with the kit's functions.

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:31.271
Running it is a preview that saves nothing.

00:02:31.271 --> 00:02:32.752
A person looks, and approves.

00:02:33.172 --> 00:02:36.459
The server runs the same command again on the newest model,

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:37.655
with its own code.

00:02:37.655 --> 00:02:39.448
It never trusts the client's result.

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:43.252
If nobody else changed a field it records, it commits.

00:02:43.252 --> 00:02:45.620
If someone did, it refuses by name.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.974
Some commands record none.

00:02:48.617 --> 00:02:52.407
Say an agent builds a command on an older revision: move one wall.

00:02:52.827 --> 00:02:56.248
Meanwhile Priya, in another session, already moved that same wall.

00:02:56.668 --> 00:03:00.255
The server re-runs the move, sees she wrote the wall's start, and

00:03:00.255 --> 00:03:02.946
refuses: Priya changed this wall while you were editing.

00:03:03.366 --> 00:03:04.967
Now both set the thickness.

00:03:04.967 --> 00:03:08.809
That command records no field, so both commit, the later one wins,

00:03:08.809 --> 00:03:10.090
and nobody is told.

00:03:12.008 --> 00:03:12.861
Chapter four.

00:03:12.861 --> 00:03:14.566
Scans, renders and claims.

00:03:19.008 --> 00:03:23.181
Pascal Editor keeps a building as a tree: site, building, levels, then

00:03:23.181 --> 00:03:24.572
walls, windows and rooms.

00:03:24.992 --> 00:03:28.402
It is the only editor here that lets an agent work beside

00:03:28.402 --> 00:03:32.380
a scan, as a reference layer, through tools like create room and add door.

00:03:33.758 --> 00:03:37.297
But in the open editor a scan is a mesh file you

00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:41.426
place by hand, and a phone capture's mesh holds at most twenty thousand faces.

00:03:41.426 --> 00:03:42.901
Not a building-scale point cloud.

00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:48.996
At the far end sits vork, an engine an agent writes as code.

00:03:48.996 --> 00:03:52.131
Its million birds render in about ninety seven seconds.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.919
The Blender times are projections from eight frames.

00:03:56.358 --> 00:03:58.458
Reading the code took some shine off the posts.

00:03:58.947 --> 00:04:02.245
Mutiny's kernel still declares walls, beams and columns.

00:04:02.245 --> 00:04:07.191
What it drains, on a ratchet, is mechanical, electrical and plumbing vocabulary.

00:04:07.611 --> 00:04:12.168
Tested at two hundred and fifty thousand elements means a performance gate with budgets.

00:04:12.168 --> 00:04:15.749
A hundred and fourteen of its three hundred and thirty five

00:04:15.749 --> 00:04:17.051
rows are still pending.

00:04:17.471 --> 00:04:21.586
And none of these tools turns a registered point cloud into building elements.

00:04:23.058 --> 00:04:26.176
So the tool with a real building model cannot see a scan

00:04:26.176 --> 00:04:29.554
yet, and the one that sees a scan stops at a reference layer.

00:04:29.554 --> 00:04:32.153
The step between is still a person with a prompt.

00:04:33.858 --> 00:04:34.559
To recap.

00:04:34.559 --> 00:04:36.663
Trades as add-ons on one kernel.

00:04:36.663 --> 00:04:40.169
Moves refused by name, while the later thickness simply wins.

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:42.624
Scans still stop at the model step.

00:04:43.044 --> 00:04:45.162
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:45.162 --> 00:04:45.767
I'm Figaro.

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:46.070
Bye!
